A||N||6||vinnywolfe||grand am brake upgrade problems||||9||
Z||000000||vinnywolfe||07-01-2000||05:53 PM||||i've read several articles on the internet about upgrading the rear braakes on a fiero to the front brakes on a 1990 grand am.  supposedly, it's a direct bolt on.  i decide to do this to my 87 gt. i bought the calipers, pads and rotors for a 1990 grand am (w/ performance package) and put them on.  the calipers didn't seem like they were centering when they went on.  when i finally forced everything together, and put the wheel and tire on, the thing was locked solid!  you couldn't force the wheel to move.  after i filed some material off of the pads i tried again.  this felt a little loser, but it was still grinding.  <BR>after i decided to try and take the car for a spin (there was a grinding noise in the rear the whole time) i got back and found that the bottom of the outer brake pad was wearing a grove in the hub of the rotor (like a lathe).  <BR>was my mistake buying the hardware for the grand am with the performance package?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||BBBAD GT||07-02-2000||12:22 AM||||I put 88 GA brakes all around with no problems.  Sorry to hear your troubles, hope you work it out.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||phillip||07-02-2000||12:36 AM||||what change was made to grand am rotors in '92? jc whitney has drilled and slotted rotors for '92- and up grand ams.<BR>would they work?<BR>didn't i also hear that s-10 blazer rotors would work too?'82-'96, they have those drilled and slotted also.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||07-02-2000||10:17 AM||||I think they changed something in '90.  Randy Agee has done this.  I'll try to find his site.  He explains everything.  Try this.  I can't remember if this is Randys but it should still help you out.  <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html</A>   <BR><P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000004||vinnywolfe||07-02-2000||06:54 PM||||thanks badd! i went and bought everything new for the rear (calipers, rotors and pads).<BR>it bolts on perfect!  i still haven't had a chance to drive it with the new brakes yet, (i'm having a hell of a time with putting on the front manifold gasket), but i'm looking forward to it.<P>THANKS again,<P>vinny<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000005||batboy||07-03-2000||09:12 PM||||I think I remember hearing that you should stay with the late 80's Grand Am brakes. I used 88 model year Grand Am brake parts too and it was perfect. You are supposed to grind a little off the caliper mounting pad, and modify and bend back the backing shield. Hopefully you did those things and I'm just stating the obvious. The Randy Agree article is the one I followed and should be considered the Grand Am to Fiero brake swapping "bible".||||reg||1
Z||000006||FIEROCIOUS||07-04-2000||03:40 AM||||I used 91 baretta brakes they are the same as the Grand Am breaks but use a better slide pin system. They changed in 92.<BR>||||reg||1
